# The feedback service's direct route, `POST /client/reports`, on the
# public site a client signed in to no gateway reports to
# (docs/feedback.md §"Straight from a client").
#
# Directives for the feedback service's own site block. Only the direct
# route is passed on here; the read routes and the web UI are the operator's
# to expose or not (behind their own handle, a VPN, or loopback only), and
# nothing in this file opens them.
#
# Install (once, as root):
#
#   install -m644 scripts/server/feedback-direct.caddy /etc/caddy/baylee-feedback-direct.caddy
#
# and in the service's site block (a file under /etc/caddy/sites/), import
# it before any catch-all, so the two never both answer one request:
#
#   feedback.baylee.acevik.de {
#       import /etc/caddy/baylee-feedback-direct.caddy
#       handle {
#           respond 404
#       }
#   }
#
# then `caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile` and
# `systemctl reload caddy`. The service needs FEEDBACK_DIRECT_KEY set (else
# the route answers 503) and FEEDBACK_TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.1, so its
# per-address allowance counts the client's address and not Caddy's.
# Caddy replaces any X-Forwarded-For a client sends with the address it
# saw, as long as no `trusted_proxies` is configured for this server.
#
# No `log` here, on purpose: the service keeps no reporter's address, and
# an access log would.

@baylee_direct_report path /client/reports
handle @baylee_direct_report {
	# The service's own bound is 6.46 MiB (`direct::MAX_BODY_BYTES`: 1 MiB
	# of client details, a 4 MiB record in base64, and room); anything
	# larger stops here before the service reads a byte of it.
	request_body {
		max_size 7MiB
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:28780
}
